<h2>The rival narratives</h2>
<p>The parties married in June 2017. On the husband's version, the wife stayed barely a week in the matrimonial home, refused to join him at his places of posting, visited only briefly in 2018, demanded Rs. 50 lakh after his father's retirement, and finally left in June 2020, never to return. On the wife's version, she was pushed out by dowry-related harassment — she had lodged a criminal case to that effect — and remained willing to resume married life with her husband and in-laws.</p>
<p>The Family Court at Deoghar believed neither cruelty nor desertion had been proved and dismissed the husband's suit in January 2024. The High Court, on first appeal, agreed.</p>

<h2>How the Division Bench tested the grounds</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Cruelty — particulars and corroboration.</strong> The alleged Rs. 50 lakh demand, threats and humiliation were not established by cogent and convincing evidence; two supporting witnesses lacked personal knowledge, and the husband admitted he had never complained to a panchayat or the police.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The criminal case — neutral at this stage.</strong> The wife's pending criminal case neither proved her allegations nor the husband's counter-narrative. Each side's matrimonial case had to stand on its own evidence.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Desertion — animus not shown.</strong> With a genuine dispute over why the wife lived apart — expulsion on her version, abandonment on his — the husband failed to prove that she left without reasonable cause intending to end cohabitation permanently.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Appellate restraint.</strong> A first appellate court interferes only where findings are contrary to the evidence; the Family Court's appraisal disclosed no such perversity.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The husband's declaration that he would not take the wife back even on a written undertaking proved costly: it undercut his claim of having sought to preserve the marriage and coloured the court's reading of both grounds.</p>
</div>

<h2>The doctrine beneath the outcome</h2>
<p>Three settled propositions carried the day. First, matrimonial relief is proved, not presumed: the petitioner's burden under Section 13 does not lighten because the marriage has broken down in fact. Second, cross-pollination between criminal and matrimonial proceedings is impermissible shorthand — a 498A case (now Section 85 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023) is not evidence of cruelty by the husband, and its mere pendency is equally not evidence of cruelty by the wife. Each forum decides on its own record. Third, desertion is a state of mind as much as a state of affairs: physical separation for years does not amount to desertion where the separation is attributable to the petitioner's own conduct or where the respondent's willingness to return is on record.</p>
<p>For practitioners, the judgment is also a lesson in witness selection. Supporting witnesses who were not present at the matrimonial home, and who depose to what they were told, add volume but not weight; the absence of any contemporaneous complaint — to family elders, a panchayat or the police — leaves an allegation of sustained cruelty hanging in the air.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Plead cruelty with dates, places and witnesses who can speak from personal knowledge; contemporaneous complaints and records matter more than recollection at trial.</p>
<p>Do not rely on the existence of criminal proceedings, in either direction; prove the underlying facts in the matrimonial trial itself.</p>
<p>For desertion, build the two-year timeline carefully and be ready to prove the respondent's intention to abandon — letters, notices and the response to a restitution offer are the usual materials.</p>
<p>Watch the petitioner's own conduct: an offer to resume cohabitation refused outright can decide an appeal.</p>
</div>
